{
  "id": "default_vessel",
  "label": "Dimenship",
  "notes": "The vessel the shell starts on, built to the GDD's production layer: one global Resource Storage in the middle as a star hub, Matter Reactors and Factories joined to it both ways, and Mission Docks joined to storage and to nothing else. Factory interconnects are authored unbuilt — the hold-star is what carries intermediates until the player builds those links. The vessel opens quiet: no standing production or haul except the extractor's out-line. Missions do not exist, so the vessel starts stocked with the Matter Mix they would have recovered. Two things here are deliberately inert: a Matter Reactor separates and draws power but makes none, and a Mission Dock has no schematic at all.",

  "energyCapacity": 10000,

  "hold": "resource_storage",

  "storages": [
    {
      "id": "resource_storage",
      "archetype": "global_hold",
      "nameOverride": null,
      "placement": { "column": 2, "row": 2, "badge": "2" },
      "initial": [
        { "item": "matter_mix", "quantity": 3600000 },
        { "item": "basic_metals", "quantity": 40000 }
      ],
      "notes": "The vessel starts stocked rather than empty: the chain has to be running when the player first looks at it, and with no missions there is nowhere else the opening Matter Mix could come from. 3,600,000 against the 500 a tick the two reactors separate is two operational hours before the shortage bites."
    },

    {
      "id": "extractor_buffer",
      "archetype": "facility_buffer",
      "nameOverride": "Extractor Buffer",
      "initial": [],
      "notes": "A facility's buffer has no cell of its own. It is drawn inside the card of the facility that works it, so a route ending at the buffer ends at that facility - which is what keeps a vessel of nine facilities from drawing eighteen cards to say the same thing."
    },
    {
      "id": "reactor_a_buffer",
      "archetype": "facility_buffer",
      "nameOverride": "Reactor Alpha Buffer",
